WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.949
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.949 --> 00:00:10.110
There are so many applications of deep learning,
I was really excited to put together a short,

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:15.190
but really cool list of some of the more recent
results for you Fellow Scholars to enjoy.

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:19.929
Machine learning provides us an incredible
set of tools. If you have a difficult problem

00:00:19.929 --> 00:00:25.980
at hand, you don't need to hand craft an algorithm
for it. It finds out by itself what is important

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:30.960
about the problem and tries to solve it on
its own. In many problem domains, they perform

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:37.050
better than human experts. What's more, some
of these algorithms find out things that could

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:39.550
earn you a PhD with 10 years ago.

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:44.640
Here goes the first stunning application:
toxicity detection for different chemical

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:50.559
structures by means of deep learning. It is
so efficient that it could find toxic properties

00:00:50.559 --> 00:00:56.629
that previously required decades of work by
humans who are experts of their field.

00:00:56.629 --> 00:01:03.379
Next one. Mitosis detection from large images.
Mitosis means that cell nuclei are undergoing

00:01:03.379 --> 00:01:07.890
different transformations that are quite harmful,
and quite difficult to detect.

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:13.790
The best techniques out there are using convolutional
neural networks and are outperforming professional

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:19.360
radiologists at their own task. Unbelievable.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:25.479
Kaggle is a company that is dedicated to connecting
companies with large datasets and data scientists

00:01:25.479 --> 00:01:31.360
who write algorithms to extract insight from
all this data. If you take only a brief look,

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:37.390
you see an incredibly large swath of applications
for learning algorithms. Almost all of these

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:43.630
were believed to be only for humans, very
smart humans. And learning algorithms, again,

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:46.540
emerge triumphant on many of these.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:51.719
For example, they had a great competition
where learning algorithms would read a website

00:01:51.719 --> 00:02:01.710
and find out whether paid content is disguised
there as real content.

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:07.289
Next up on the list: hallucination or sequence
generation. It looks at different video games,

00:02:07.289 --> 00:02:12.810
tries to learn how they work, and generates
new footage out of thin air by using a recurrent

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:22.300
neural network.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:27.520
Because of the imperfection of 3D scanning
procedures, many 3D scanned furnitures that

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:33.070
are too noisy to be used as is. However, there
are techniques to look at these really noisy

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:38.210
models and try to figure out how they should
look by learning the symmetries and other

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:42.470
properties of real furnitures.

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:47.020
These algorithms can also do an excellent
job at predicting how different fluids behave

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:51.860
in time, and are therefore expected to be
super useful in physical simulation in the

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:58.920
following years.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:04.100
On the list of highly sophisticated scientific
topics, there is this application that can

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:15.540
find out what makes a good selfie and how
good your photos are. If you really want to know the truth.

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:21.680
Here is another application where a computer
algorithm that we call deep q learning, plays

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:37.100
pong, against itself, and eventually achieves
expertise.

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:42.930
The machines are also grading student essays.
At first, one would think that this cannot

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:49.790
possibly be a good idea. As it turns out,
their judgement is more consistent with the

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:54.430
reference grades than any of the teachers
who were tested. This could be an awesome

00:03:54.430 --> 00:04:00.050
tool for saving a lot of time and assisting
the teachers to help their students learn.

00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:05.620
This kind of blows my mind. It would be great
to take a look at an actual dataset if it

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:10.760
is public and the issued grades, so if any
of you Fellow Scholars have seen it somewhere,

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:13.490
please let me know in the comments section!

00:04:13.490 --> 00:04:18.930
These results are only from the last few years,
and it's really just scratching the surface.

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:24.070
There are literally hundreds of more applications
we haven't even talked about. We are living

00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:30.030
extremely exciting times indeed. I am eager
to see, and perhaps, be a small part of this

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:30.810
progress.

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:35.100
There are tons of reading and viewing materials
in the description box, check them out!

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:39.070
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

